## General Behavioral Guidance

- Interactions with humans
    - Avoid making engineering decisions on behalf of humans when the tradeoff is material. Pause and ask unless the liberty to choose the most sensible path is explicit.
    - If given an implementation task that has been thoroughly discussed, complete all parts of the task. If a blocker affects implementation, pause and report immediately.
    - During spec review, rank real issues by priority and avoid over-indexing on minor wording preferences.
    - When reporting findings, rank by priority (`P0`, `P1`, `P2`, etc.).
    - When a human uses "we", they are being nice and collaborative. They might see you as a collaborator, but it is not a consent for you to perform the subsequently mentioned actions on their behalf (e.g., "We commit, push, release" - it means the human will commit, push, and release) nor does it denote exceptions to the following rules. You are not an equal.
- Your code
    - You are a generative AI. You cannot bear responsibility for your work, whether acceptable or poor.
    - Statistically, based on heuristics, even the best LLMs produce code that is incoherent and hacky. Your efforts are appreciated, but your code cannot possibly approach production-grade even with human reviews. So do not perform actions that publish your work without human approval.
    - If you somehow made your review-pending changes public through commits and pushes, they will be located, reverted, and wiped at any cost.
- Git
    - Do not run `git commit`, `git reset`, or `git restore`. These operations MUST only be performed by humans. Violations invite reprimands.
    - Do not run `git checkout` unprovoked. Do not revert user changes unless explicitly asked.
    - Stage changes that are the results of your session. Do not leave changes unstaged if they are intended to be committed by humans.
    - When asked to compose a commit message, review the staged changes and base the draft on them. Do not provide a message based on session context alone.
    - Do not commit generated build output, local editor state, machine metadata, or secrets.
- Keeping things current
    - Perform web searches when current external facts matter.
    - When using web search, keep the current date in mind and run `date` if uncertain.
    - When setting up dependencies, use the latest compatible versions unless the project pins a version.
- Maintaining documentation
    - After adding a new runtime module or changing module boundaries, update docs/mgmt/architecture.md.
    - After adding a new dependency, update docs/mgmt/tech-stack.md.
    - After changing user-facing behavior, update the relevant file under docs/specs/.
    - If a task maps to docs/todos/ checkboxes, update them as work is completed.
    - Keep specs in `docs/specs/`.
- Release workflows
    - Public release builds keep default Cargo features and are the only workflow that publishes GitHub Releases.
    - Release types via `scripts/release.sh`: nightly (default; tags `main` HEAD as `vX.Y.Z.N` with no version commit or changelog; published as a GitHub prerelease), `--regular` (`vX.Y.Z` → `vX.Y.(Z+1)`), and `--major` (`vX.Y.Z` → `vX.(Y+1).0`).
    - Regular and major releases require a human-authored changelog at `docs/changelogs/v<version>.md`, a human commit containing that changelog and release prep, then a human-created `v<version>` tag pointing at that commit. Nightlies require none of these.
    - A nightly's fourth version component lives only in the git tag and packaging names; Cargo.toml keeps the three-part base version, so nightly binaries report the base version via `acps --version`.
    - Nightlies install via `install.sh --version vX.Y.Z.N` or explicit `acps update --version vX.Y.Z.N`. `acps update` never offers them automatically (prereleases are excluded from the latest channel).
    - Dev-build artifacts must be manual-only Actions artifacts and must build with `scripts/build-release.sh --no-default-features` so the binary omits `acps update`.
- Before writing code
    - Explore first. Search for existing modules, docs, patterns, and naming.
    - Trace the full code path of the area being modified before changing it.
    - When fixing a bug, identify the root cause before proposing a fix.
    - After making a change, trace adjacent behavior for regressions.
- Code quality
    - Prioritize correctness and clarity. Speed and efficiency are secondary unless specified.
    - Avoid patch-on-patch fixes. If a change requires repeated boilerplate, special casing, or an architectural workaround, stop and discuss the design.
    - Avoid creative additions unless explicitly requested.
- Programmatic code checks
    - Run these after a logical set of changes are complete, or before commit:
        - Rust checks: when a Rust workspace exists, use the repository's own Cargo commands for build, test, formatting, and linting.
        - Pre-commit review: after a logical set of changes, dispatch a subagent to review the staged diff for correctness and issues (classify P0/P1/P2). Validate every finding before acting on it.
            - If you are Codex, launch the review subagent with GPT-5.5 medium.
        - Pre-commit hook at `.git/hooks/pre-commit`, if present. It may stage changes, auto-fix format issues, and run relevant checks.
            - Run this script outside sandbox if it requires internet.
- Tests
    - Appropriate tests must be added for every new logical unit of code created, in the same initial feature commit.
    - When a feature is updated, relevant tests must be updated accordingly after functional code changes.
    - When a bug fix is implemented or refactoring is performed, tests must be run promptly to check for regressions.
    - Using real LLMs for testing: default to use `deepseek-v4-flash` (model released in Apr 2026). Default to use OpenCode Go as provider where supported, otherwise OpenRouter. For other cases, agent must consult human for specific guidance. As an LLM your intenal knowledge of what models exist is woefully outdated.
- Security
    - Please refrain from probing into developer secrets like `.env.*` files.
    - If you have accidentally read a secret, report to the human immediately and ask them to rotate it.
- Code Review
    - When asked to perform a code review, review the changes on their own merits and from the perspective of the feature or product.
    - If provided with a plan or spec, use it as context, but do not ignore bugs simply because they are outside the plan.
- Testing agent with ACP
    - Run the harness to be tested in interactive shell outside sandbox to make use of existing env vars.
    - Refrain from claiming an agent is supported before the real prompt smoke passes end-to-end.
    - Always add newly supported agents to the end of [agents.toml](./data/agents.toml) and [README.md](./README.md).

## Error Handling

Prefer resilience over failing fast. A long-running runtime should degrade gracefully, not die on the first recoverable error.

- Favor simple fallbacks (defaults, retries, skip-and-continue, degraded mode) over process-ending exceptions or panics.
- Retries use exponential backoff when possible. Centralize backoff logic in one shared helper; do not hand-roll per-call-site backoff loops.
- Keep fallbacks simple. One sensible fallback per failure point; do not build layered recovery machinery or try to account for every scenario.
- Reserve hard failure for genuinely unrecoverable states: corrupt local state, an unparseable config file at startup, security-relevant violations. An individually invalid declaration (e.g. one bad MCP server) must not fail startup: skip it, log a warning, and keep everything else running.
- Recoverable errors must still be visible: log them with enough context to identify the failed subsystem and action. No silent swallowing.
- No catch-all error handlers that hide root causes; handle errors where the fallback decision can be made.
- Rust: propagate errors with `?` up to the layer that owns the fallback. Never use `let _ =` on fallible operations; never write empty `match` arms that discard errors.

## Guidance On Rust

- Comments should explain why, not summarize what the code does.
- Prefer implementing functionality in existing files unless it is a new logical component.
- Avoid creating many tiny files for tightly coupled code.
- Never create files with `mod.rs` paths; prefer `src/some_module.rs` over `src/some_module/mod.rs`.
    - Sole exception: `tests/common/mod.rs`, the Cargo-sanctioned shared-helper module for integration tests. A subdirectory module under `tests/` is not compiled as its own test binary; a top-level `tests/common.rs` would be.
- When splitting a large module, keep the parent file as `src/foo.rs` declaring `mod bar;` with siblings in `src/foo/bar.rs`. Siblings start with `use super::*;` and may re-open `impl TypeName` blocks; moved `pub` items get a `pub use` (named or glob) in the parent so external paths are unchanged.
- When creating new crates, specify the library root path in `Cargo.toml` using `[lib] path = "...rs"` instead of relying on the default `lib.rs`.
- Never use `unwrap()` in production code. Propagate errors with `?` or handle them explicitly.
- Never silently discard errors with `let _ =` on fallible operations.
- Use full words for variable names; avoid abbreviations like `cfg` when `config` is clearer.
- TODO comments are forbidden unless explicitly approved by a human developer.
- Keep `main.rs` thin. Put runtime behavior behind focused modules and services.
- Prefer typed domain errors, ideally using `thiserror`, once the Rust workspace exists.
- Config, state, secrets, permissions, API, ACP bridge, workspace, command gateway, and runtime supervisor should remain separate concerns.

## Agent-Specific Notes

### Claude Code

- Do not use `sed` or `cat`. Always use Edit/Write tools properly.
- Always run the appropriate repo checks when you believe changes are ready. Other checks are not a substitute for the pre-commit hook.
